    {
      "@type": "ScholarlyArticle",
      "@id": "https://yassiralkarawi.github.io/research/quantum-cognitive-radar-thermal-loss.html#article",
      "headline": "Quantum Cognitive Radar: Adaptive Detection With Entanglement Under Thermal-Loss Channels",
      "name": "Quantum Cognitive Radar: Adaptive Detection With Entanglement Under Thermal-Loss Channels",
      "description": "An adaptive quantum-cognitive detection framework that studies entanglement-assisted radar operation under realistic thermal-loss channels. The study asks how a radar can adapt its detection strategy when attenuation and thermal background noise change. It combines entangled signal and idler modes with feedback control, connecting quantum illumination to an adaptive sensing problem rather than treating the transmitter settings as fixed. A two-mode squeezed-vacuum transmitter, joint idler–signal receiver and quantum neural-network controller form the proposed system. A Gaussian correlation detector is compared with the quantum Chernoff bound, while the controller adjusts operating parameters under an energy constraint. Hardware-aware simulations report approximately 3 dB advantage and up to 40% shorter integration time at a false-alarm probability of 0.05. At a false-alarm probability of 0.001, reported detection probability is 0.47, versus 0.20 for the classical comparator. These are simulation results for the paper's channel and receiver assumptions, not measurements from a deployed radar.",
      "datePublished": "2026",
      "dateModified": "2026-09-07",
      "inLanguage": "en",
      "creativeWorkStatus": "Published",
