    {
      "@type": "ScholarlyArticle",
      "@id": "https://yassiralkarawi.github.io/research/high-pass-filter-overshoot-bessel-gaussian.html#article",
      "headline": "Mitigating in Band Overshoot in Digital High-Pass Filters: Design and Analysis of Bessel and Gaussian Filters for Maximally Flat Step Response",
      "name": "Mitigating in Band Overshoot in Digital High-Pass Filters: Design and Analysis of Bessel and Gaussian Filters for Maximally Flat Step Response",
      "description": "Design and analysis of Bessel and Gaussian high-pass filters to reduce in-band overshoot while preserving a maximally flat step response. The paper examines a filter-design trade-off: a flat time-domain step response does not automatically imply a flat amplitude–frequency response. It compares high-pass designs derived from Bessel and Gaussian low-pass prototypes. Bilinear transformations generate the IIR high-pass designs. A parallel structure combines a direct path with a low-pass branch. Mathcad numerical evaluations compare filter orders and parallel versus stage-by-stage connections, considering both transient behaviour and frequency-response overshoot. Gaussian-based designs show smaller negative step overshoot in the evaluated examples, while frequency-response overshoot increases with order. The proposed parallel scheme reports up to 30% lower amplitude–frequency overshoot than the conventional schemes without degrading transient response. The comparisons are numerical filter-design results, not measured performance of every hardware implementation.",
      "datePublished": "2025-09-30",
      "dateModified": "2026-09-07",
      "inLanguage": "en",
      "creativeWorkStatus": "Published",
